1
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,980
A sprinter crosses the line.

2
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:12,030
The timing system measures 10.386 seconds.

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:15,280
But the big screen only shows 10.4 seconds.

4
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:17,340
It has been rounded.

5
00:00:17,390 --> 00:00:21,340
What would the screen show with 2 digits after the point?

6
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:24,990
Pause the video and work it out.

7
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,250
Keep 2 digits after the point: 10.38.

8
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:30,560
The next digit is 6.

9
00:00:30,610 --> 00:00:33,190
6 is 5 or more, so we round up.

10
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:34,550
The 8 becomes 9.

11
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:38,170
The screen would show 10.39 seconds.

12
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:45,470
On a number line, 10.386 is past the halfway point, so it is closer to 10.39.

13
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,960
You already know how to round whole numbers.

14
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:52,290
Round 3,748 to the nearest hundred.

15
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:55,230
It lies between 3,700 and 3,800.

16
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:59,090
The tens digit is 4, which is less than 5.

17
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:02,210
So it rounds down, to 3,700.

18
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:05,720
You are always told how accurate the answer must be.

19
00:01:05,770 --> 00:01:08,350
This is the degree of accuracy.

20
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:13,640
It might be the nearest whole number, 1 decimal place, or 2 decimal places.

21
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:16,450
We write d p for decimal places.

22
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:18,300
The rule is always the same.

23
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:21,680
First, find the last digit you are keeping.

24
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:23,580
Then look at the next digit.

25
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:25,680
We call it the decider.

26
00:01:25,730 --> 00:01:28,320
If the decider is 5 or more, round up.

27
00:01:28,370 --> 00:01:32,120
If it is less than 5, leave the digit as it is.

28
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:34,970
Round 10.386 to 1 decimal place.

29
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:35,950
Keep the 3.

30
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,610
The decider is 8, so round up.

31
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:40,850
The answer is 10.4.

32
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,840
Now to 2 decimal places.

33
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:43,740
Keep the 8.

34
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:46,220
The decider is 6, so round up.

35
00:01:46,270 --> 00:01:48,420
The answer is 10.39.

36
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:49,260
Your turn.

37
00:01:49,310 --> 00:01:51,840
Round 12.75 to 1 decimal place.

38
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:55,480
Pause the video and try it.

39
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:58,470
Keep 1 digit after the point: 12.7.

40
00:01:58,520 --> 00:01:59,920
The decider is 5.

41
00:01:59,970 --> 00:02:02,050
5 or more means round up.

42
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:04,220
The answer is 12.8.

43
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:05,540
Here is the method.

44
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:12,400
Step 1: count that many digits after the decimal point, and draw a line after the last one.

45
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:16,250
Step 2: look at the digit just after the line.

46
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:20,640
Step 3: if it is 5 or more, add 1 to the last digit you kept.

47
00:02:20,690 --> 00:02:23,130
If it is under 5, leave it.

48
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:28,030
Step 4: write exactly the number of decimal places asked for.

49
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,000
Round 27.649 to 1 decimal place.

50
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:36,000
Keep 1 digit after the point, and draw the line.

51
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:37,400
The decider is 4.

52
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:40,370
That is less than 5, so the 6 stays.

53
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:42,490
The answer is 27.6.

54
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:43,870
Do not look at the 9.

55
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,510
Only the digit straight after the line matters.

56
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:51,220
Round 3.596 to 2 decimal places.

57
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:52,960
Keep 2 digits: 3.59.

58
00:02:53,010 --> 00:02:56,910
The decider is 6, so we add 1 to the 9.

59
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,230
3.59 plus 0.01 is 3.60.

60
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:04,610
The 9 becomes 0, and 1 is carried to the tenths.

61
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:05,510
Write 3.60.

62
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:07,150
Keep the final zero.

63
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:12,140
It shows that the number is given to 2 decimal places.

64
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:13,010
Your turn.

65
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:15,880
Round 0.0458 to 2 decimal places.

66
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:19,190
Pause and work it out.

67
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:22,260
Keep 2 digits after the point: 0.04.

68
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:24,820
The decider is 5, so round up.

69
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:26,990
The answer is 0.05.

70
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:30,250
Three friends share a 25 dollar bill equally.

71
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:33,990
How much does each pay, to the nearest cent?

72
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:39,810
25 divided by 3 is 8.3333, and the threes go on for ever.

73
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:42,160
Cents need 2 decimal places.

74
00:03:42,210 --> 00:03:43,000
Keep 8.33.

75
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:45,690
The decider is 3, so round down.

76
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:48,760
Each friend pays 8 dollars 33.

77
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:51,920
Eli rounds 8.2471 to 1 decimal place.

78
00:03:51,970 --> 00:03:54,860
Which line goes wrong first?

79
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:55,970
Line 1 is wrong.

80
00:03:56,020 --> 00:04:00,140
Eli started from the last digit and rounded again and again.

81
00:04:00,190 --> 00:04:04,370
Only the digit straight after the kept place matters.

82
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:05,100
Keep 8.2.

83
00:04:05,150 --> 00:04:07,760
The decider is 4, so the 2 stays.

84
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:09,220
The answer is 8.2.

85
00:04:09,270 --> 00:04:11,790
Always round in one step.

86
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:15,720
Sprint times are recorded to 2 decimal places.

87
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:22,840
The men's 100 metres world record is 9.58 seconds, set by Usain Bolt in 2009.

88
00:04:22,890 --> 00:04:26,660
To the nearest whole number, that is 10 seconds.

89
00:04:26,710 --> 00:04:30,240
To 1 decimal place, it is 9.6 seconds.

90
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:34,740
The more decimal places you keep, the more accurate the number is.

91
00:04:34,790 --> 00:04:38,360
That is why races use hundredths of a second.

92
00:04:38,410 --> 00:04:39,770
One last question.

93
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:44,310
Round 6.997 to 2 decimal places, and write all the digits.

94
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:47,820
Pause the video and answer.

95
00:04:47,870 --> 00:04:48,910
Keep 6.99.

96
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,940
The decider is 7, so add 1 hundredth.

97
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:55,450
6.99 plus 0.01 is 7.00.

98
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:59,050
Write 7.00, with both zeros.

99
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:05,070
To remember: find the last digit to keep, then look at the next digit, the decider.

100
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:06,580
5 or more, round up.

101
00:05:06,630 --> 00:05:08,190
Under 5, keep.

102
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:11,750
Round in one step, from the original number.

103
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:17,570
Give exactly the number of decimal places asked for, and keep any final zero.

104
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:22,750
Great work! Now practise lesson 3.2 on Camday.
